    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only real test class; the other two are base/support classes). `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked to define membership level 4 as requiring tenure of more than 365 days, with a new owner (zero tenure) never exceeding level 3.\n\n**`AssignOwnerMembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 replaced the opaque `Math.min(level, 3)` hard cap with an explicit tenure factor:\n- Added a named constant `TENURE_THRESHOLD_DAYS = 365`.\n- The level now gains `+1` when `tenureDays(owner) > 365`, where tenure is `ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(registrationDate, LocalDate.now())`, clamped at 0 and treated as 0 when no registration date is recorded.\n- Because a newly created owner's `registrationDate` is the current date (set by `BuildOwner`/`BusinessDays`), its tenure is 0, so the level-4 factor never fires at creation \u2014 a new owner with an email + `namesakeCount` of 0 lands at exactly 3, matching the acceptance test. Updated the Javadoc (now \"1 to 4\") to state the rule.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipLevel` schema: `maximum` 3 \u2192 4 and rewrote the description to document the tenure factor instead of \"capped at 3 (level 4 reserved for tenure)\".\n\nThis mirrors the codebase's existing date-derivation pattern (`OwnerAgeBand` using `registrationDate` as the temporal anchor), so the rule is a genuine definition rather than a magic cap.",
